    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogies. Let's get back to the technical aspects.\n\nThe inconsistent behavior you're seeing is likely due to the Time To Live (TTL) of the DNS records. TTL is the amount of time, in seconds, that a DNS server caches the DNS record for. When you update the A records, the changes are sent to the DNS servers with a TTL, which determines how long the servers will cache the new information.\n\nIf the TTL is too low, the changes may not propagate quickly enough to all the DNS servers, causing inconsistencies in the DNS resolution. On the other hand, if the TTL is too high, the changes may take too long to propagate, causing delays in the DNS resolution.\n\nIn your case, it's possible that the TTL of the A records is too low, causing some DNS servers to cache the old information for a longer period than others. This would result in some users seeing the old site while others see the new site.\n\nTo troubleshoot this issue, you can try increasing the TTL of the A records to a higher value, such as 1 hour or 2 hours, to see if that resolves the issue. Alternatively, you can try using a DNS server",
